## Reading/writing files

python can read from and write to files sitting on disk, letting a program save data outside of just running once and losing everything when it closes
basic idea: open a file, do something with it, then close it again so nothing gets left dangling
there are a couple of main modes for text files, covered properly under open() below

## open()

the built-in function used to open a file and get a file object back, which is what you actually read from or write to
needs a filename, and a mode telling it what you plan to do with the file: f = open("notes.txt", "r") r means read
f = open("notes.txt", "w") w means write, overwrites the whole file if it already exists
f = open("notes.txt", "a") a means append, adds to the end instead of overwriting
reading the contents once it's open: f = open("notes.txt", "r")
content = f.read()
print(content)
f.close()
writing works similarly: f = open("notes.txt", "w")
f.write("hello file")
f.close()
forgetting to close a file can cause problems, especially with writing, since changes might not actually get saved to disk until it's closed properly

## Context managers

a cleaner way of handling something that needs setup and cleanup, files being the most common example
using with automatically closes the file for you, even if an error happens partway through, so you never have to remember to call close() manually
example: with open("notes.txt", "r") as f:
content = f.read()
print(content)
as soon as the code exits the with block, the file gets closed automatically behind the scenes
this is the pattern worth reaching for basically every time, instead of manually calling open() and close() yourself

## pathlib

a more modern way of working with file paths, built around a Path object instead of plain strings
example: from pathlib import Path p = Path("notes.txt")
p.exists() result True or False, checks if it's actually there
p.name result "notes.txt"
p.suffix result ".txt"
makes joining paths together far less error prone than manually gluing strings with slashes, since it works the same on windows and mac/linux even though they use different slash characters normally: folder = Path("documents")
full_path = folder / "notes.txt"

## Directories

a folder, basically, used to organise files
pathlib handles most directory tasks cleanly: from pathlib import Path Path("new_folder").mkdir() creates a folder
Path("new_folder").exists() checks if it exists
list(Path(".").iterdir()) lists everything in the current folder
os.listdir() and os.makedirs() are the older, pre-pathlib way of doing the same sort of thing, still common in existing code

## File manipulation

covers the general tasks of moving, renaming, copying, and deleting files from within python itself
the shutil and os modules handle most of this: import shutil
import os os.rename("old_name.txt", "new_name.txt")
shutil.copy("notes.txt", "notes_backup.txt")
os.remove("notes_backup.txt")
worth being careful with anything that deletes or overwrites — python won't ask for confirmation first, it just does it

## JSON

a common text format for storing structured data, looks a lot like a python dictionary
python's json module converts between JSON text and actual python objects
example: import json data = {"name": "Ben", "age": 20} with open("data.json", "w") as f:
json.dump(data, f) with open("data.json", "r") as f:
loaded = json.load(f) print(loaded)
result {'name': 'Ben', 'age': 20}
dump writes python data out as JSON, load reads JSON back in as python data
dumps and loads, with an s on the end, do the same thing but work with plain strings directly instead of files

## CSV

comma separated values, a plain text format for tabular data, basically a simple spreadsheet
python's csv module handles reading and writing it properly, including tricky edge cases like commas sitting inside a field
example: import csv with open("people.csv", "r") as f:
reader = csv.reader(f)
for row in reader:
print(row)
writing works the same way in reverse: with open("people.csv", "w") as f:
writer = csv.writer(f)
writer.writerow(["name", "age"])
writer.writerow(["Ben", 20])
DictReader and DictWriter do the same job but let you work with each row as a dictionary using the header row as keys, instead of a plain list by position

## Regular expressions

a pattern language for searching, matching, and manipulating text, accessed through the re module
looks cryptic at first but covers a huge amount of ground once it clicks
example: import re text = "my number is 12345"
match = re.search(r"\d+", text)
print(match.group())
result 12345
a few building blocks worth knowing: \d matches any digit
\w matches any letter, digit, or underscore
means one or more of whatever came before it
means zero or more
. matches any character
also useful for replacing text: re.sub(r"\d+", "X", "my number is 12345")
result "my number is X"

## Modules

a single python file containing code, functions, classes, variables, that can be reused in another file
literally just any .py file — the moment you write one, it's technically a module
example, a file called helpers.py containing: def greet(name):
return "hello " + name

## Imports

how you actually bring a module's code into another file so you can use it
example, assuming helpers.py exists in the same folder: import helpers
helpers.greet("Ben")
can also import just one specific thing from a module instead of the whole thing: from helpers import greet
greet("Ben")
or rename something on the way in if it's long or clashes with something else: import helpers as h
h.greet("Ben")

## Packages

a folder containing multiple related modules, grouped together as one importable unit
turns a plain folder into a package by including a special file inside it, covered next
example structure: my_package/
init.py
helpers.py
utils.py
once set up, you can import from inside it like: from my_package import helpers

## __init__.py

a special, usually empty file that tells python to treat that folder as a package
can also be used to control what gets exposed when someone imports the package, or to run setup code the moment the package gets imported
older python needed this file present to recognise a folder as a package at all — newer python is more lenient about it, but including it is still the standard, expected convention

## __name__

a special built-in variable that every python file automatically has, holding the name of the current module
if the file is being run directly, __name__ gets set to the literal string __main__
if the file is instead being imported into another file, __name__ gets set to the module's actual filename instead
this is what lets a single file behave differently depending on whether it's the one being run, or just being borrowed for its functions

## __main__

the value __name__ holds specifically when a file is run directly rather than imported
the common pattern you'll see constantly: def main():
print("running the program") if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
this means main() only runs when the file is executed directly, not when some other file imports it just to reuse its functions — without this check, importing a file would also accidentally run all its top-level code immediately

## Project structure

how files and folders get organised once a program grows past a single script
a reasonably typical layout for a small project: my_project/
main.py
my_package/
init.py
helpers.py
utils.py
tests/
test_helpers.py
README.md
requirements.txt
keeping related code grouped into packages, tests separated out, and dependencies listed in one place makes a project far easier to navigate as it grows, both for you later on and for anyone else looking at it